{"command": "sed -n '1,120p' /testbed/specs/001-transactions/spec.md", "exit_code": 0, "stderr": "", "stdout": "# Feature Specification: Tagged Cache (TaggedCache)\n\n**Feature Branch**: `001-transactions`\n\n**Created**: 2026-10-17\n\n**Status**: Draft\n\n**Input**: User description: \"Build cachetools.tagged.TaggedCache(maxsize), using the existing cachetools.LRUCache for bounded storage and preserving all existing cachetools APIs. Milestone 1 (R01-R03) retained; Milestone 2 adds R04-R06; Claim-A candidate extensions (put_no_recency_refresh 2026-09-21; resize expiry preservation 2026-09-22) recorded.\"\n\n## User Scenarios & Testing *(mandatory)*\n\n### User Story 1 - Store and retrieve tagged entries (Priority: P1)\n\nA developer creates a bounded cache that associates each stored value with a\nset of string tags. They insert entries, retrieve values, and rely on\nisolation from the originals.\n\n**Why this priority**: Core value of the feature; without store/retrieve the\ntype is useless. This is the MVP slice.\n\n**Independent Test**: Create the cache with a small capacity, insert one\nentry with tags, read it back, and confirm the returned value equals the\nstored value but is a separate object (mutating the returned value or the\noriginal does not affect the other). Also confirm a missing key raises\n`KeyError`.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** an empty cache, **When** `put(key, value, tags)` is called with\n   a finite iterable of string tags, **Then** the entry is stored and\n   `get(key)` returns a value equal to the input value.\n2. **Given** a stored entry whose value is a mutable object, **When**\n   `get(key)` is called, **Then** the returned object is a detached deep\ncopy: mutating it (and mutating the original after re-put) does not affect\n   the cached or returned counterpart.\n3. **Given** a cache, **When** `get(missing_key)` is called, **Then**\n   `KeyError` is raised and nothing else changes.\n4. **Given** a cache, **When** `put` is called with a bare string as\n   `tags`, or with a tag entry that is not a string, **Then** `ValueError`\nis raised and the cache state (entries, tags, recency) is unchanged.\n5. **Given** duplicate tags in the iterable, **When** `put` is called,\n   **Then** the entry is stored with each tag only once.\n\n---\n\n### User Story 2 - Invalidate by tag and evict with clean state (Priority: P2)\n\nA developer removes all entries carrying a tag in one call and relies on\nbounded eviction to never leak stale tag memberships.\n\n**Why this priority**: Tag-based bulk removal is the distinguishing\ncapability over the plain bounded cache; eviction hygiene is required for\ncorrectness under capacity pressure.\n\n**Independent Test**: Fill a small cache, call `invalidate(tag)`, and verify\nthe returned count and that exactly the tagged entries are gone. Then force\neviction by overfilling, re-put the evicted key with new tags, and verify\nthe old tags no longer match it.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** entries with and without tag `t`, **When** `invalidate(t)` is\n   called, **Then** exactly the entries carrying `t` are removed and the\n   return value equals the number removed.\n2. **Given** an existing key stored with old tags, **When** `put` is called\n   for the same key with new tags, **Then** the old tag memberships are\n   removed (invalidating an old tag no longer removes the entry) and the\n   value is replaced.\n3. **Given** a full cache, **When** a new entry is inserted and the\n   least-recently-used entry is evicted, **Then** the evicted key's tag\n   memberships no longer affect later reuse of that same key.\n4. **Given** a cached entry, **When** `get(key)` is called, **Then** the\n   entry becomes the most-recently-used for eviction order.\n5. **Given** any sequence of operations, **When** `len(cache)` is called,\n   **Then** it equals the number of live entries (never counting evicted or\n   invalidated entries).\n\n---\n\n### User Story 3 - Safe construction and replacement (Priority: P3)\n\nA developer constructs the cache with a capacity and expects invalid input\nto fail fast and atomically.\n\n**Why this priority**: Guard rails that prevent silent corruption; important\nbut secondary to the primary store/retrieve/invalidate flows.\n\n**Independent Test**: Attempt construction with invalid capacities and\nattempt a replacement with invalid tags; verify errors are raised and\nexisting state is untouched.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** capacity input that is not a positive integer (zero, negative,\n   non-integer, or a boolean), **When** the cache is constructed, **Then**\n   `ValueError` is raised.\n2. **Given** a stored entry, **When** `put` for the same key is called with\n   invalid tags, **Then** the old value, old tag memberships, and old\n   recency position are all unchanged.\n3. **Given** any value object, **When** it is stored, **Then** it consumes\n   exactly one capacity slot regardless of its size or complexity.\n\n---\n\n### User Story 4 - Bulk invalidation with any/all semantics (Priority: P2)\n\nA developer removes many entries at once by several tags, choosing whether\nan entry must carry *any* of the tags or *all* of them, and gets back the\nnumber of distinct entries removed.\n\n**Why this priority**: Bulk tag operations are the main scaling path for\ntag-based cleanup; they build directly on the P1/P2 tag machinery.\n\n**Independent Test**: Seed entries with overlapping tag sets, call the\nbulk-invalidation method in each mode, and verify exactly the expected\nentries are gone and the returned count equals the number of distinct\nremoved keys.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n"}